After the morning rush, it is finally quiet.
Elif and I lean against the back counter.
We take a break and drink the coffee.
Elif smiles at me.
"You did that really well today," she says.
"My first coffee was a disaster," I reply.
She laughs and shakes her head.
"It is completely normal," she says.
"It simply takes time to learn to make good espresso."
"The second coffee was already much better," I say proudly.
"Exactly," she nods.
We chat comfortably about the neighbourhood.
"The association is planning a summer festival soon," Elif explains.
"We are organizing music and food for the whole street."
That sounds like a wonderful event.
"Can I maybe help with that too?" I ask.
"Of course!"
"We always need volunteers," she says happily.
I finish drinking my coffee and am very content.
